# Unit Testing Guidelines

Test individual functions and modules in isolation. Place tests in the same file using `#[cfg(test)]`.

## Related Rules

This rule works together with:

- `.cursor/rules/rust-code-style.mdc` - Code style patterns (formatting, imports, assertions with `pretty_assertions`)
- `.cursor/rules/project-structure.mdc` - Project structure and file locations
- `.cursor/rules/error-handling.mdc` - Error handling patterns (when testing error scenarios)
- `.cursor/rules/rust-qa-loop.mdc` - QA validation process (after writing/modifying tests)

## Basic Structure

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    use pretty_assertions::assert_eq;

    #[test]
    fn test_function_name() {
        // Test implementation
    }
}
```

## Using rstest for Parametrized Tests

**Prefer `rstest` for testing multiple inputs:**

```rust
use rstest::*;
use pretty_assertions::assert_eq;

#[rstest]
#[case("$1,234.56", Some(1234.56))]
#[case("999.99", Some(999.99))]
#[case("0.99", Some(0.99))]
#[case("R$ 199.99", Some(199.99))]
#[case("", None)]
#[case("N/A", None)]
fn test_parse_price_string(#[case] input: &str, #[case] expected: Option<f64>) {
    assert_eq!(parse_price_string(input), expected);
}

#[rstest]
#[case::basic("hello world", "hello world")]
#[case::newlines("hello\nworld", "hello world")]
#[case::tabs("hello\tworld", "hello world")]
#[case::multiple_spaces("hello   world", "hello world")]
#[case::leading_trailing("  hello world  ", "hello world")]
#[case::empty("", "")]
fn test_normalize_string(#[case] input: &str, #[case] expected: &str) {
    assert_eq!(normalize_string(input), expected);
}
```

## Integration tests (HTTP)

For HTTP-level tests in this repo, prefer `apps/*/tests/*.rs` with `keydock_testkit::test_app()`
and `axum_test::TestServer` (see `apps/keydock/tests/health.rs` for the established pattern).

## Test Organization

Group related tests with descriptive comments:

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    use pretty_assertions::assert_eq;

    #[test]
    fn test_basic_cases() {
        // Basic functionality
        assert_eq!(parse_price_string("$1,234.56"), Some(1234.56));
    }

    #[test]
    fn test_edge_cases() {
        // Edge cases and boundaries
        assert_eq!(parse_price_string("123.45.67"), Some(123.45));
        assert_eq!(parse_price_string("."), None);
    }

    #[test]
    fn test_invalid_inputs() {
        // Invalid inputs should return None
        assert_eq!(parse_price_string("N/A"), None);
        assert_eq!(parse_price_string("Free"), None);
    }
}
```

## Helper Functions

Create helpers to reduce duplication:

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::AmazonPriceParser;
    use scraper::Html;

    fn parse_price_from(html: &str) -> Option<f64> {
        let document = Html::parse_document(html);
        AmazonPriceParser::new(&document, "hash").parse().unwrap()
    }

    #[test]
    fn parses_combined_whole_and_fraction() {
        let html = r#"
            <span class="a-price aok-align-center">
                <span class="a-price-symbol">R$</span>
                <span class="a-price-whole">52<span class="a-price-decimal">,</span></span>
                <span class="a-price-fraction">37</span>
            </span>
        "#;

        assert_eq!(parse_price_from(html), Some(52.37));
    }

    #[test]
    fn returns_none_when_no_price() {
        assert_eq!(parse_price_from(r#"<div>no price</div>"#), None);
    }
}
```

## Async Tests with Mocking

```rust
use wiremock::{Mock, MockServer, ResponseTemplate};

#[tokio::test]
async fn test_scraper() {
    let test_data = include_str!("_data/amazon.txt");
    let mock_server = MockServer::start().await;
    let base_url = mock_server.uri().parse().unwrap();
    let client = ScraperClient::new().unwrap();

    Mock::given(wiremock::matchers::method("GET"))
        .and(wiremock::matchers::path("/dp/B08N5WRWZL"))
        .respond_with(ResponseTemplate::new(200).set_body_string(test_data))
        .mount(&mock_server)
        .await;

    let url = ProductUrl::from_str("https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08N5WRWZL")
        .unwrap()
        .with_base_url(base_url)
        .unwrap();

    let scraper = AmazonScraper::new(&client);
    let product = scraper.scrape(url).await.unwrap();

    assert_eq!(product.title, "Expected Title");
    assert_eq!(product.price, Some(305.99));
}
```

## Best Practices

- **Use `rstest`** for parametrized tests with `#[case]`
- **Use `pretty_assertions`** for better error messages
- **Use `expect()` freely**: `expect()` can be used freely in tests and test support code for clearer error messages
- **Group tests** by functionality (basic, edge cases, invalid)
- **Create helpers** to reduce duplication
- **Use `include_str!()`** for large fixtures
- **Test error cases** explicitly
- **Keep tests focused** on one behavior
